---

## 2.2 Typography

Use:

* **Inter** for all primary UI text

Typography should feel modern, clear, and product-focused.

Preferred text styles:

Page title:

```text
text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight
```

Section title:

```text
text-lg font-semibold
```

Card title:

```text
text-sm font-medium
```

Body text:

```text
text-sm text-foreground
```

Secondary body text:

```text
text-sm text-muted-foreground
```

Caption / metadata:

```text
text-xs text-muted-foreground
```

Large stat number:

```text
text-2xl font-semibold
```

Rules:

* do not use too many font sizes
* hierarchy should come from weight, spacing, and placement more than giant text jumps
* avoid overly bold UI everywhere
* titles should feel compact and clean, not oversized

---

## 2.3 Radius, Borders, and Shadows

Preferred radius:

```text
rounded-xl
```

Small elements:

```text
rounded-md
```

Buttons / inputs:

```text
rounded-lg
```

Preferred border:

```text
border border-border
```

Preferred shadow:

```text
shadow-sm
```

Rules:

* keep shadows subtle
* rely on borders and spacing more than dramatic shadows
* avoid large soft floating-card styles for every component
* cards should feel crisp and modern

---

## 2.4 Spacing System

Use a generous but controlled spacing system.

Common layout spacing:

```text
gap-6
space-y-6
p-6
px-6
py-6
```

Compact internal spacing:

```text
gap-3
gap-4
space-y-3
space-y-4
```

Dense metadata spacing:

```text
gap-2
text-xs
```

Rules:

* do not cram content
* use whitespace to create hierarchy
* dashboards and boards should breathe
* avoid inconsistent padding between similar components

---

# 3. Layout Standards

## 3.1 App Shell

The app uses a modern SaaS shell:

```text
Sidebar
Top Header
Main Content Area
Optional Right Drawer / Sheet
```

Rules:

* sidebar is fixed or stable on desktop
* header stays clean and compact
* main content should feel spacious
* avoid nested containers that make the UI feel boxed in too many times

---

## 3.2 Sidebar

Sidebar style should be minimal and product-like.

Sidebar includes:

* logo / product identity
* main navigation
* workspace switcher if needed
* settings entry
* user/workspace footer area if needed

Recommended width:

```text
w-64
```

Sidebar styling:

* white or near-white background
* subtle right border
* small clean icons
* compact labels
* active state using muted primary background or subtle accent
* no heavy gradients
* no oversized branding area

Rules:

* navigation should feel calm and easy to scan
* do not over-style the sidebar
* active nav item should be obvious but not loud
* use Lucide icons consistently

---

## 3.3 Top Header

Top header may include:

* page title
* search
* workspace/project context
* notifications
* user menu
* primary action button if needed

Rules:

* keep the header light and uncluttered
* do not pack too many controls into one row
* page-level actions should be aligned consistently
* search should feel integrated, not oversized

---

## 3.4 Main Content Area

Main content should follow a consistent width and spacing rhythm.

Rules:

* use `p-6` as the default page padding
* keep page sections separated with `space-y-6`
* use grid layouts for dashboards where helpful
* avoid random width changes between pages
* pages should feel like part of one product family

---

# 4. Component Standards

## 4.1 General Component Philosophy

Use shadcn-vue components whenever appropriate.

Components should feel:

* crisp
* neutral
* accessible
* reusable
* minimal

Rules:

* do not visually reinvent the same component in multiple ways
* prefer a single strong component style over many inconsistent variants
* reusable components must remain visually aligned

---

## 4.2 Buttons

Button system should stay restrained.

Primary button:

* use for main page action
* solid primary color
* compact but clear

Secondary button:

* muted or outline style
* use for supportive actions

Ghost button:

* use for lightweight actions in dense UI

Destructive button:

* use only for destructive actions

Rules:

* do not put too many primary buttons on the same screen
* one main action per area should dominate
* destructive actions should always be visually distinct
* button sizes should stay consistent across the app

---

## 4.3 Cards

Cards are a core UI building block.

Cards should be used for:

* stats
* project summaries
* panels
* recent activity
* table wrappers
* settings sections

Card style:

* white background
* light border
* subtle shadow
* rounded corners
* generous padding

Rules:

* cards should separate content clearly
* do not over-stack card-inside-card-inside-card layouts
* prefer simple, strong panels

---

## 4.4 Tables

Tables are important for:

* projects
* members
* tasks (list view)
* notifications/logs if needed

Table style should be:

* clean
* highly readable
* light borders
* muted headers
* compact but not cramped

Rules:

* use tables for structured data, not everything
* rows should have good vertical rhythm
* status badges and avatars should fit cleanly within rows
* avoid overly dense spreadsheet-like design

---

## 4.5 Badges

Badges are used for:

* status
* priority
* role
* labels/tags

Rules:

* badges should be compact
* use muted variants when possible
* use strong color only when semantic meaning matters
* do not create too many badge styles

Examples:

* status badge
* priority badge
* role badge
* label badge

---

## 4.6 Avatars

Avatars are important in:

* task cards
* member lists
* comments
* activity timeline

Rules:

* avatar sizes should be consistent
* use avatar stacks for multiple assignees
* do not overcrowd cards with too many visible avatars
* member presence should feel informative, not noisy

---

## 4.7 Dialogs / Drawers / Sheets

Use dialogs or sheets for:

* create/edit forms
* confirmations
* task details
* quick actions

Rules:

* task details should preferably use a right-side drawer/sheet
* destructive actions require confirmation dialog
* forms inside dialogs must remain well-spaced and readable
* avoid oversized full-screen modals unless necessary

---

## 4.8 Forms

Forms must be clean and highly usable.

Use:

* Input
* Textarea
* Select
* Combobox / Command
* Checkbox
* Radio
* Switch
* Date picker if needed

Rules:

* forms should always include clear labels
* required fields should be visually indicated consistently
* show validation errors inline
* group related fields together
* do not overload users with giant unstructured forms
* long forms should be broken into sections

---

## 4.9 Empty States

Every major list or content area should have a meaningful empty state.

Empty states should include:

* short clear title
* one-line explanation
* primary CTA when relevant

Rules:

* empty states must feel intentional
* avoid generic "No data" without guidance
* keep empty states simple and product-like

---

## 4.10 Loading States

Loading must be polished.

Use:

* skeletons
* spinner only for small inline states
* subtle pending indicators on buttons

Rules:

* avoid blank pages while loading
* use skeleton cards/tables for main page loads
* use button loaders for actions
* do not show giant blocking spinners for every action

---

# 5. Dashboard Standards (Stripe-style)

The dashboard should feel like **Stripe / Vercel / Supabase**.

Dashboard sections may include:

* top stat cards
* recent activity
* my tasks
* active projects
* upcoming deadlines
* team summary

Rules:

* dashboard should be structured and calm
* stat cards should be concise and readable
* avoid overloading the dashboard with too many widgets
* charts should only be used where they genuinely help
* recent activity should feel like a polished product feed

Dashboard layout should typically use:

* top row stats
* middle content grid
* bottom detail sections

Stat cards should include:

* title
* value
* optional trend / context
* small supporting text

---

# 6. Board Standards (Linear-style)

The board is the core experience and should feel **Linear-like**.

Columns should be:

* clean
* spacious
* easy to scan
* clearly separated
* not overly colorful

Use typical columns like:

* Backlog
* Todo
* In Progress
* Review
* Done

Rules:

* column headers should be compact and strong
* column width should feel balanced
* avoid heavy card decoration
* focus on readable task information
* board interactions should feel fast and lightweight

---

## 6.1 Task Cards

Task cards should contain only useful information.

Preferred content:

* task title
* label(s) if relevant
* assignee avatars
* due date if present
* priority indicator
* small metadata only

Rules:

* do not overload task cards with excessive details
* task title should remain the main focus
* metadata should feel secondary and compact
* task cards should look calm, not noisy

---

## 6.2 Board Interactions

Board interactions should support:

* drag and drop
* quick create task
* open task details drawer
* hover actions if useful
* keyboard-friendly navigation if implemented later

Rules:

* interactions must feel smooth
* hover states should be subtle
* active drag states should be obvious but clean
* avoid flashy animation

---

# 7. Task Details Standards

Task details should open in a right-side drawer or sheet.

Drawer sections may include:

* title
* description
* assignees
* labels
* due date
* priority
* subtasks
* attachments
* comments
* activity

Recommended tabs:

* Description
* Subtasks
* Attachments
* Activity

Rules:

* task details should feel structured and premium
* content should be split clearly into sections
* metadata controls should be easy to update
* discussion/activity should feel collaborative and readable

---

# 8. Page-Specific Standards

## 8.1 Projects Page

Projects page may support:

* list view or card view
* search
* filters
* create project action

Rules:

* use clean card or table presentation
* project name and status should be easily scannable
* progress and team information should be secondary but visible
* filters should not overpower the content

---

## 8.2 Members Page

Members page should feel structured and trustworthy.

Display:

* avatar
* name
* email
* role
* status
* actions

Rules:

* role/status should use restrained badges
* actions should use dropdowns where appropriate
* membership management should feel clean and safe

---

## 8.3 Notifications Page

Notifications should be simple and highly readable.

Rules:

* clear unread vs read state
* concise notification text
* optional timestamp
* easy mark-as-read interactions
* no visual clutter

---

## 8.4 Settings Page

Settings should be broken into clear sections such as:

* Workspace Settings
* Members
* Notifications
* Account

Rules:

* settings pages should use card sections
* destructive actions should be clearly separated
* form sections should not feel crowded
* keep settings calm and structured

---

# 9. Iconography

Use:

* **Lucide icons**

Rules:

* use one icon system across the app
* icon size should be consistent
* do not mix icon styles
* icons should support meaning, not decoration overload

Recommended icon size patterns:

```text
h-4 w-4
h-5 w-5
```

---

# 10. Motion and Interaction

Animations should be subtle and purposeful.

Allowed motion style:

* soft hover transitions
* light fade/scale for dropdowns/dialogs
* subtle panel transitions
* smooth drag interactions

Rules:

* avoid dramatic bounce animations
* avoid flashy motion for productivity UI
* movement should support clarity, not entertainment

Preferred transition feel:

* quick
* smooth
* understated

---

# 11. Responsive Behavior

The UI must remain usable on smaller screens.

Rules:

* sidebar may collapse on smaller widths
* tables should degrade gracefully
* boards may become horizontally scrollable
* drawers and dialogs should adapt for smaller screens
* important actions must stay accessible

Do not design only for large desktop screens.

---

# 12. Accessibility Standards

Basic accessibility is required.

Rules:

* maintain sufficient text contrast
* use semantic buttons and inputs
* ensure focus states are visible
* dialogs/sheets must handle focus correctly
* forms must have labels
* icon-only buttons must have accessible labels

---

# 13. Consistency Rules

Before creating new UI:

* check whether a similar component already exists
* reuse component patterns whenever possible
* follow the same spacing, radius, and typography rules
* do not introduce one-off page styles without strong reason

Consistency is more important than novelty.

---

# 14. Things to Avoid

Do not generate UI that feels like:

* a generic bootstrap admin template
* a loud marketing website
* an old enterprise panel
* a colorful student dashboard
* a cramped CRUD app

Avoid:

* too many colors
* oversized shadows
* inconsistent padding
* random border radius changes
* too many button variants
* decorative gradients
* overly dense task cards
* giant hero sections inside app pages
* multiple competing focal points on one screen

---

# 15. Definition of Good UI in This Project

Good UI in this project means:

* it feels like a startup SaaS product
* it is calm and professional
* information is easy to scan
* the layout breathes
* actions are obvious
* boards are clean and fast
* dashboards are structured and premium
* components feel unified
* it looks ready for real users

---

# 16. Final Design Rule

Whenever generating UI for this project, prefer:

* **Linear-style boards**
* **Stripe-style dashboards**
* **shadcn-vue components**
* **neutral surfaces**
* **minimal color palette**
* **Inter typography**
* **subtle borders and shadows**
* **clean startup SaaS polish**

If there is a choice between "more decorative" and "more product-like," always choose **more product-like**.
